  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.08,{x}_{2}=8.68$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-1.2$ and ${z}_{2}=2.3,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{\u00af}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{\u00af}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{\u2015}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.08=\\overset{\u00af}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n8.68=\\overset{\u00af}{x}+2.3\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.68-3.08 & =(2.3-(-1.2))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.6 & =3.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{\u00af}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.08 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{\u00af}{x}-1.92 \\\\ \\overset{\u00af}{x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
